Hi Marc,
Thanks for your suggestion. I don't fully understand how to organize the data frame for occu() in R and still pair the relevant covariate data with observations. I have tried to get my data formatted for a single season analysis using csvToUMF but all covariates are converted to factors. Converting the data from long to wide format for the purpose of stacking is challenging since I'm working with the output of an access database query and I'm new to manipulating data frames. See HHBObs_Night_Season.csv

In my analysis using colext() I do have "season" as a factor (in order to contrast seasonal estimates) but it too gets stored as a yearly site covariate (using formatMult) and I can't model psi if it is not a site covariate, correct? I get this error message again:
> fm2 <- colext(~season, ~1, ~1, ~1, Bat)
Error in eval(expr, envir, enclos) : object 'season' not found

Additionally, I find that when I model colonization and extinction with "jdate" (a cosine transformation of Julian date) I get the same error where jdate is not found. If I use "julian" instead the error is :
 > fm32 <- colext(~1, ~julian, ~julian, ~1, Bat)
Error in model.frame.default(gamformula, yearlySiteCovs, na.action = NULL) :
  object is not a matrix

I have attached the two different csv files I use  for occu() and  colext(), as well as the reassignment for formatMult and R code here. I haven't found (or properly understood) tutorials on how to correctly handle the necessary data frame manipulation, but I'm still researching. Thanks again for your help.
Cheers,
Rachel

On Fri, Feb 17, 2017 at 8:29 AM, Kery Marc <marc...@vogelwarte.ch> wrote:
> Dear all,
>
> have you considered using what Andy calls "stacking the years" ? That is, treat the primary seasons as independent, stack their data all on top of each other and fit a static occupancy model using occu() with primary season as a factor (if you want to contrast all years) or as a continuous explanatory variable (if you want to estimate a trend).
>
> As I see it, you commit a tiny bit of pseudoreplication (treating observations from same site across different primary occasions as independent), but that's probably not so bad.
>
> Best regards  --- Marc
>
